<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Artuqids of Harput</h1><p class="st-meta">1112 CE – 1234 CE · capital Harput</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A branch of the Artuqid dynasty at the fortress of Harput, where Belek Ghazi held the King of Jerusalem captive.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a>, a fortress near the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqid</a> rule early in the 12th century. Its most famous holder was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Belek Ghazi</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>, who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-i-count-of-edessa">Joscelin I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">County of Edessa</a> in 1122 and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> in 1123 and imprisoned them there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Armenian sympathisers briefly seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> castle to free the captives in 1123, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Belek</a> retook it. He was killed besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manbij">Manbij</a> in 1124. Harput then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-hasankeyf">Artuqids of Hasankeyf</a> until a separate line was established in 1185 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-abu-bakr">Imad al-Din Abu Bakr</a>. The small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> built mosques, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput-ulu-mosque">Ulu Cami of Harput</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Squeezed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> branch was annexed by the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> in 1234.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1112 CE – 1234 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.83%;width:2.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1112 CE – 1234 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Harput</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Belek Ghazi, Imad al-Din Abu Bakr</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Artuqids of Kharput</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1173&amp;focus=artuqids-of-harput" aria-label="Artuqids of Harput on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5529 772 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Artuqids of Harput lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6034 1077h0M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5849 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5849 1028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=1173&amp;focus=artuqids-of-harput">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
